namespace Velum.Studio;

/// <summary>Every action Studio's catalog has, one value each. <see cref="ActionTypes"/> has their ids and descriptions.</summary>
public enum ActionType
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Switch scene (<c>scene.switch</c>): Puts a scene on air.
    /// </summary>
    SceneSwitch,
    /// <summary>
    /// Next scene (<c>scene.next</c>): Puts the scene after the one on air on air, in the Scenes list's order (wrapping around).
    /// </summary>
    SceneNext,
    /// <summary>
    /// Previous scene (<c>scene.previous</c>): Puts the scene before the one on air on air, in the Scenes list's order (wrapping around).
    /// </summary>
    ScenePrevious,
    /// <summary>
    /// Pin the vertical canvas (<c>vertical.pin</c>): Keeps the vertical canvas on one scene while the main (landscape) canvas switches scenes, or lets it follow the scene on air again. pin keeps it on the scene; follow lets it follow the scene on air (no scene needed); toggle pins it to the scene, or lets it follow again when it's already pinned there (with no scene, a pinned canvas follows again). A scene with no vertical layout shows its horizontal layout, fitted. Needs the vertical canvas (Settings › Video › Vertical canvas); while it's off, the choice is kept for when it's back on. If the pinned scene is deleted, the vertical canvas follows the scene on air again.
    /// </summary>
    VerticalPin,
    /// <summary>
    /// Show or hide a layer (<c>layer.visibility</c>): Shows, hides or toggles one layer of a scene.
    /// </summary>
    LayerVisibility,
    /// <summary>
    /// Restart a layer (<c>layer.restart</c>): Plays a layer's show again from its start: its show transition, and a video or animation it shows from its first frame. A hidden layer is shown. (A hide then a show, however close together, does the same.)
    /// </summary>
    LayerRestart,
    /// <summary>
    /// Freeze when its scene comes on air (<c>layer.freeze_on_air</c>): Turns a layer's "Freeze when this scene comes on air" setting on, off, or the other way: while on, the layer shows the picture it had when its scene came on air, and the same source stays live everywhere else.
    /// </summary>
    LayerFreezeOnAir,
    /// <summary>
    /// Copy a layer's style to another (<c>layer.copy_style</c>): Gives one layer the look of another, as Copy style and Paste style do: its border (with or without its colors), corners, effects, crop, size and place, blend or show and hide transitions. One undo step; parts the other layer can't take are left out.
    /// </summary>
    LayerCopyStyle,
    /// <summary>
    /// Layer opacity (<c>layer.opacity</c>): Sets how see-through a layer is, 0 (gone) to 100 (solid), or moves it by a step (for dials). Its picture, border and glow fade together. Give exactly one of percent or step.
    /// </summary>
    LayerOpacity,
    /// <summary>
    /// Clip to the layer below (<c>layer.clip</c>): Turns a layer's "Clip to layer below" on, off, or the other way: while on, the layer shows only where the layer under it has a picture (Photoshop's clipping mask).
    /// </summary>
    LayerClip,
    /// <summary>
    /// Turn an effect on or off (<c>filter.toggle</c>): Turns one effect (filter) on a source or scene on, off, or the other way.
    /// </summary>
    FilterToggle,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set an effect's setting (<c>filter.set</c>): Sets one setting of an effect (filter): a number, on or off, a color, a choice or a file, checked against that effect's own settings (catalog.list has them under filter_settings). A number can also move by a step (for dials) or fade to its new value. Give value or step.
    /// </summary>
    FilterSet,
    /// <summary>
    /// Apply a framing (<c>layer.framing</c>): Moves a layer to one of its framings (a saved position, size and crop), animated as the framing says, from wherever the layer is now.
    /// </summary>
    LayerFraming,
    /// <summary>
    /// Nudge a layer (<c>layer.nudge</c>): Moves a layer a step from where it is (dx pixels right, dy pixels down; negative for left or up), gliding there from wherever it is on screen. Every press moves it another step, as far as you like, and a press during a move adds to that move.
    /// </summary>
    LayerNudge,
    /// <summary>
    /// Mute (<c>audio.mute</c>): Mutes, unmutes or toggles a source's sound.
    /// </summary>
    AudioMute,
    /// <summary>
    /// Volume (<c>audio.volume</c>): Sets a source's volume in dB or on the mixer's 0 to 100 fader scale, or moves it by a step in dB (for dials). Give exactly one of db, percent or step_db.
    /// </summary>
    AudioVolume,
    /// <summary>
    /// Monitor (<c>audio.monitor</c>): Chooses whether you hear a source in your headphones: off, headphones only, or headphones and stream.
    /// </summary>
    AudioMonitor,
    /// <summary>
    /// Monitor bus (<c>audio.monitor_bus</c>): Chooses where a monitor bus plays on this computer and its volume. Omitted settings keep their values.
    /// </summary>
    AudioMonitorBus,
    /// <summary>
    /// Monitor send (<c>audio.monitor_send</c>): Sets how much of a source is heard on one monitor bus, after its volume and mute.
    /// </summary>
    AudioMonitorSend,
    /// <summary>
    /// App volume in Windows (<c>audio.app_volume</c>): Sets how loud an application is for you: Windows' own volume for it (its Volume Mixer slider), on every process and output of the app. Not what viewers hear (that's the source's fader, audio.volume). Windows applies this volume before Velum captures the app, so the source's sound follows it unless the source ignores the app's Windows volume (its setting in the Inspector); a muted app, or one at 0, is silent there either way. Give input or executable, and percent, step or mode (none of them only reads it). Windows only.
    /// </summary>
    AudioAppVolume,
    /// <summary>
    /// Audio track (<c>audio.track</c>): Puts a source's sound on one of Studio's six tracks, takes it off, or toggles it. Recordings record the tracks chosen in Studio's recording settings.
    /// </summary>
    AudioTrack,
    /// <summary>
    /// What viewers get (<c>veloq.visibility</c>): Chooses where a source's sound goes for Velora viewers over VELoQ: public (viewers get a slider and can adjust it; in the VOD), public_drop_vod (a slider, left out of the VOD: music), hidden_keep_vod or hidden_drop_vod (heard with no slider of its own, in the VOD or not), or not_sent (never leaves Studio). toggle_vod keeps it where it is and flips it in or out of the VOD. Changes that move it to another track apply the next time you go live; a VOD flip on its own slider applies at once.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqVisibility,
    /// <summary>
    /// Where viewers start (<c>veloq.viewer_start</c>): Sets where Velora viewers' slider for a source starts (its own slider over VELoQ), and whether it starts muted. Each viewer can still change it. Give volume_percent, muted, or both.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqViewerStart,
    /// <summary>
    /// Editor+ stem (<c>veloq.stem</c>): Turns on, off or toggles recording a source as its own Editor+ stem when it's mixed with others (a send, the Stingers track, Other). Viewers never hear stems.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqStem,
    /// <summary>
    /// Put in a folder or send (<c>veloq.group</c>): Puts a source in an audio folder (it keeps its own slider, shown under the folder's name) or a send (one slider for all its sources), or takes it out when group is left out.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqGroup,
    /// <summary>
    /// Who gets a slider first (<c>veloq.slider_order</c>): Sets a source's place in who gets the 8 viewer sliders first (1 first; 0 = automatic: microphones and devices, then capture and game, then overlays, then media files).
    /// </summary>
    VeloqSliderOrder,
    /// <summary>
    /// Stinger sounds for viewers (<c>veloq.transitions</c>): Chooses where every stinger's sound goes for Velora viewers over VELoQ (the Transitions channel): hidden_keep_vod by default (heard in the Stingers track, in the VOD).
    /// </summary>
    VeloqTransitions,
    /// <summary>
    /// Smart defaults (<c>veloq.smart_defaults</c>): Gives sources their smart first choice for Velora viewers: microphones, calls, game and alerts get a slider; music gets a slider but stays out of the VOD; stingers and media files are heard with no slider. With folders, also sorts them into Voice, Game, Music and Alerts folders (made when missing). One source, or every source with sound.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqSmartDefaults,
    /// <summary>
    /// New audio folder or send (<c>veloq.create_group</c>): Makes an audio folder (its sources keep their own sliders, shown together under its name) or a send (one slider for all its sources).
    /// </summary>
    VeloqCreateGroup,
    /// <summary>
    /// Change an audio folder or send (<c>veloq.update_group</c>): Renames an audio folder or send, makes a folder a send or a send a folder, or sets whether a send is in the VOD. Give any of name, kind and send_visibility.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqUpdateGroup,
    /// <summary>
    /// Delete an audio folder or send (<c>veloq.delete_group</c>): Deletes an audio folder or send; its sources stay, out of any folder.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqDeleteGroup,
    /// <summary>
    /// Send to Velora with (<c>veloq.send_with</c>): Chooses how Studio sends to Velora: whip (one mixed track, the default) or veloq (Velora's own protocol: a track per source viewers can mix), and over VELoQ how many qualities (layers, 1 to 4; 0 = the default, 2). Applies the next time you go live.
    /// </summary>
    VeloqSendWith,
    /// <summary>
    /// Viewer sliders (<c>veloq.sliders</c>): Shows how many of the 8 viewer sliders a VELoQ Go Live gives out now (sources and sends).
    /// </summary>
    VeloqSliders,
    /// <summary>
    /// Copy a source's settings to another (<c>source.copy_settings</c>): Gives one source the settings of another of the same kind, as Copy settings and Paste settings do: two Auroras made to glow alike, a second capture card set up like the first, a title given another's font and colors. What makes each one itself stays its own (what an Aurora follows, which device or window a capture shows, a file, a title's words). One undo step.
    /// </summary>
    SourceCopySettings,
    /// <summary>
    /// Record this source separately (<c>source.record_separately</c>): Records a clean source feed beside the next program recording, when the encoder budget allows it. Options left out keep their settings; mode defaults to toggle. Audio defaults to none.
    /// </summary>
    SourceRecordSeparately,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set a source's setting (<c>source.set</c>): Sets one setting of a source: one Studio makes itself (an Aurora's follows, colors, palette, points, edges, intensity, softness, reach, drift or ease; a blur box's blur, strength, angle, block size, feather, corner radius, tint, brightness or opacity; catalog.list has them under source_settings) or a plugin's source (the settings its plugin made automatable, such as an avatar's held expression, camera or outfit; catalog.list has the loaded plugins' under plugin_settings). The source_settings topic lists every source's settings with their names, ranges and values now. A number can also move by a step (for dials); an on/off setting takes "toggle". Saved with the collection, like an Inspector edit. Give value or step.
    /// </summary>
    SourceSet,
    /// <summary>
    /// Trigger a source's action (<c>source.trigger</c>): Fires one of a plugin source's actions, as its button in the Inspector does: an avatar's "Toggle expression", for one. The settings whose kind is trigger in the source_settings topic (catalog.list's plugin_settings has their schemas). Nothing is stored: there is nothing to save or undo.
    /// </summary>
    SourceTrigger,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set text (<c>text.set</c>): Changes what a text source says.
    /// </summary>
    TextSet,
    /// <summary>
    /// Remove a source from all scenes (<c>source.remove_from_all_scenes</c>): Removes every layer of a source, on every scene and canvas (nested scenes and vertical layouts included); the source itself stays. Automations that used one of those layers are turned off. Needs "confirm": true; one undo puts it all back.
    /// </summary>
    SourceRemoveFromAllScenes,
    /// <summary>
    /// Delete a source everywhere (<c>source.delete_everywhere</c>): Deletes a source and every layer of it. Automations that used it are turned off, detectors stop watching it (one that watched only it is deleted), and Mirrors and settings that pointed at it point at nothing. Needs "confirm": true; one undo puts it all back.
    /// </summary>
    SourceDeleteEverywhere,
    /// <summary>
    /// Merge duplicate sources (<c>source.merge</c>): Merges sources of the same kind into one (the keep source's settings win): every layer, automation, detector and Mirror that used one of them uses the keeper, the others are deleted, and their names keep working as names of the keeper. Needs "confirm": true; one undo puts it all back.
    /// </summary>
    SourceMerge,
    /// <summary>
    /// Point a Mirror (<c>mirror.show</c>): Chooses what a Mirror source shows: another source with a picture, or nothing.
    /// </summary>
    MirrorShow,
    /// <summary>
    /// Media control (<c>media.control</c>): Plays, pauses, restarts, stops or seeks a media source (a video, a sound or a playlist), or moves a playlist to its next or previous item. With seek_ms and an action, it seeks first ("seek to 30 s and play"); with seek_ms alone, a paused source stays paused.
    /// </summary>
    MediaControl,
    /// <summary>
    /// Screenshot (<c>output.screenshot</c>): Saves a picture (PNG or JPEG) of what's on air, a scene, one source, or the capture(s) showing, at full resolution, in Studio's screenshot folder or one you choose.
    /// </summary>
    OutputScreenshot,
    /// <summary>
    /// Start recording (<c>record.start</c>): Starts recording what's on air to a file.
    /// </summary>
    RecordStart,
    /// <summary>
    /// Stop recording (<c>record.stop</c>): Stops the recording and saves the file.
    /// </summary>
    RecordStop,
    /// <summary>
    /// Record (<c>record.toggle</c>): Starts recording, or stops it if it's running.
    /// </summary>
    RecordToggle,
    /// <summary>
    /// Pause recording (<c>record.pause</c>): Pauses the recording, resumes it, or toggles. The file carries on in one piece when it resumes: no gap, sound and picture together, and its length leaves the paused time out.
    /// </summary>
    RecordPause,
    /// <summary>
    /// Start replay buffer (<c>replay.start</c>): Starts keeping the last seconds of what's on air in memory (as long as Studio's replay buffer setting says), ready for Save replay.
    /// </summary>
    ReplayStart,
    /// <summary>
    /// Stop replay buffer (<c>replay.stop</c>): Stops the replay buffer; what it held is let go.
    /// </summary>
    ReplayStop,
    /// <summary>
    /// Replay buffer (<c>replay.toggle</c>): Starts the replay buffer, or stops it if it's running.
    /// </summary>
    ReplayToggle,
    /// <summary>
    /// Save replay (<c>replay.save</c>): Saves the replay buffer's last seconds to an MP4, in the replay buffer's folder (Studio's recordings folder) or one you choose, named like screenshots. Returns where it went.
    /// </summary>
    ReplaySave,
    /// <summary>
    /// Go live (<c>stream.golive</c>): Goes live to every destination that's on, or to the one given. Already live, it starts the destinations that are on (or the one given) but not live yet, beside the others, and says which it started.
    /// </summary>
    StreamGolive,
    /// <summary>
    /// End stream (<c>stream.stop</c>): Ends the stream. Viewers see it end, so it needs "confirm": true; a key should also ask for a long press.
    /// </summary>
    StreamStop,
    /// <summary>
    /// Select destination (<c>destination.select</c>): Chooses where Go live sends the stream.
    /// </summary>
    DestinationSelect,
    /// <summary>
    /// Go live on a destination (<c>destination.start</c>): Starts the stream to one destination. While live, it joins the destinations already going out (the others carry on); offline, Studio goes live to it alone.
    /// </summary>
    DestinationStart,
    /// <summary>
    /// End a destination's stream (<c>destination.stop</c>): Ends the stream to one destination at once; the others carry on (ending the only one ends the stream). Its viewers see the stream end, so it needs "confirm": true.
    /// </summary>
    DestinationStop,
    /// <summary>
    /// Audio sent (<c>destination.audio</c>): Chooses what a destination's live audio is: full (the stream's full mix, the default) or vod_safe (without the sources marked Live only, such as music and alerts, which keeps them out of that platform's VOD; its live viewers don't hear them either). For platforms and custom servers; Velora takes none (it leaves Live-only sources out of its VOD itself). Applies the next time it goes live.
    /// </summary>
    DestinationAudio,
    /// <summary>
    /// Destination health (<c>destination.health</c>): Shows a destination's own state and health, while multistreaming too: offline, connecting, live, reconnecting or error (with why), with bitrate, packet loss, round trip and reconnects while live.
    /// </summary>
    DestinationHealth,
    /// <summary>
    /// Start virtual camera (<c>vcam.start</c>): Turns on the virtual camera so video-call apps can show what's on air.
    /// </summary>
    VcamStart,
    /// <summary>
    /// Stop virtual camera (<c>vcam.stop</c>): Turns off the virtual camera.
    /// </summary>
    VcamStop,
    /// <summary>
    /// Virtual camera (<c>vcam.toggle</c>): Turns the virtual camera on, or off if it's on.
    /// </summary>
    VcamToggle,
    /// <summary>
    /// Stats (<c>stats.show</c>): Shows Studio's live numbers: frames rendered and late, and while live the bitrate, packet loss and round trip; while recording, frames dropped.
    /// </summary>
    StatsShow,
    /// <summary>
    /// Viewers (<c>stream.viewers</c>): Shows how many are watching on each platform that says, while live.
    /// </summary>
    StreamViewers,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set title and category (<c>streaminfo.apply</c>): Applies a title, category and tags (any of them) to Studio's stream info and sends it to every platform lit in the Stream manager, or to the platforms given.
    /// </summary>
    StreaminfoApply,
    /// <summary>
    /// Send a chat message (<c>chat.send</c>): Sends a message to the chats lit under All in the chat panel (the same "Sends to" row), or to the platforms given. Limited to a few messages a minute per connection.
    /// </summary>
    ChatSend,
    /// <summary>
    /// Chat mode (<c>chat.mode</c>): Turns a chat mode on or off: slow mode, followers-only, subscribers-only or emote-only.
    /// </summary>
    ChatMode,
    /// <summary>
    /// Ban or time out (<c>chat.moderate</c>): Bans, unbans or times out a person in a platform's chat. Every parameter is explicit: there are no defaults.
    /// </summary>
    ChatModerate,
    /// <summary>
    /// Emote picker (<c>chat.emotes</c>): The emote picker's tabs as Studio's picker builds them (SPEC-08 §9): one per connected platform that can send and has emotes, each with its look (style), its sections in that platform's order, every emote's pictures, lock state and the text a pick inserts. With query, the search hits too. For a remote's composer.
    /// </summary>
    ChatEmotes,
    /// <summary>
    /// Delete a message (<c>chat.delete</c>): Deletes one chat message for everyone, as the Chat dock's Delete message does. The message is a chat row's native_id (from the chat topic's events or the chat view).
    /// </summary>
    ChatDelete,
    /// <summary>
    /// Clear chat (<c>chat.clear</c>): Clears a platform's chat for everyone. Viewers see every message go, so it needs "confirm": true; a key should also ask for a long press.
    /// </summary>
    ChatClear,
    /// <summary>
    /// Run an automation (<c>automation.run</c>): Runs an automation now, whatever its trigger (its Run button).
    /// </summary>
    AutomationRun,
    /// <summary>
    /// Turn an automation on or off (<c>automation.enable</c>): Turns an automation on (it runs when its trigger fires), off, or the other way.
    /// </summary>
    AutomationEnable,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set a variable (<c>variable.set</c>): Sets an automation variable: what {var:name} shows in a text layer and an automation's If can check. Empty forgets it. Kept by Studio across restarts.
    /// </summary>
    VariableSet,
    /// <summary>
    /// Avatar lip sync (<c>avatar.lipsync</c>): Turns a lip-sync pairing on or off: a voice moving an avatar's mouth in an avatar app over VMC (Settings › Avatars). Off, the avatar's mouth closes and Studio stops sending to it. Needs Avatars turned on (Settings › Advanced › Avatars).
    /// </summary>
    AvatarLipsync,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set a Dynamic Sources variable (<c>dynamic.set_variable</c>): Sets a variable Dynamic Sources choose by: the category or the title (the date and time is the computer's clock). This changes what Dynamic Sources show without changing the stream's category or title on any platform.
    /// </summary>
    DynamicSetVariable,
    /// <summary>
    /// Switch scene collection (<c>collection.switch</c>): Opens another scene collection, as Studio's collection menu does: its scenes, sources and automations take the place of the open one's.
    /// </summary>
    CollectionSwitch,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set the transition (<c>transition.set</c>): Sets the transition a scene switch plays (unless the incoming scene has its own), how long it takes and whether it plays reversed, as the transition picker, duration and Reverse next to Take do. Give a type, the name of a saved transition, a duration, reverse, or any of them together.
    /// </summary>
    TransitionSet,
    /// <summary>
    /// Wait until the transition finishes (<c>transition.wait</c>): Answers once the scene transition playing now has finished (after a moment's look when none is playing, so a switch sent just before is waited for). A controller holds the presses after it until then: switch, wait, then show a layer.
    /// </summary>
    TransitionWait,
    /// <summary>
    /// Set a scene's transition (<c>scene.transition_override</c>): Sets the transition that plays whenever a scene comes on air (its "transition into this scene"), in place of the default; "default" clears it.
    /// </summary>
    SceneTransitionOverride,
    /// <summary>
    /// Studio mode (<c>studio.mode</c>): Turns studio mode on, off, or the other way: on, a preview scene sits next to what's on air, edits happen there, and Take puts it on air with the transition. Turning it on previews the scene on air.
    /// </summary>
    StudioMode,
    /// <summary>
    /// Preview a scene (<c>scene.preview</c>): Puts a scene in studio mode's preview, for the next Take (turning studio mode on if it's off). What's on air doesn't change.
    /// </summary>
    ScenePreview,
    /// <summary>
    /// Take (<c>studio.take</c>): In studio mode, puts the preview scene on air with the transition into it; what was on air becomes the preview, so a second Take goes back.
    /// </summary>
    StudioTake,
    /// <summary>
    /// Draw scenes on (<c>gpu.draw_on</c>): Chooses the graphics card Studio draws its scenes on (Settings › Graphics cards), apart from the one it encodes on: auto (what Studio picks itself) or a card. Applies at once when nothing is streaming or recording (the preview, the virtual camera and a replay buffer hold their picture for about a second while it moves), else as soon as the stream and recording have stopped. Studio keeps the choice; a card that's gone later is drawn on as auto, said once.
    /// </summary>
    GpuDrawOn,
    /// <summary>
    /// Encode on (<c>gpu.encode_on</c>): Chooses the graphics card whose hardware encoder (NVENC, AMD AMF, Intel Quick Sync, VA-API) every stream and recording encodes with (Settings › Graphics cards), apart from the card that draws: auto (the first hardware encoder that opens, where its driver puts it), software (x264 on the CPU) or a card. Applies to each stream and recording started from then on; ones running keep their encoder. A destination set to its own encoder keeps it.
    /// </summary>
    GpuEncodeOn,
    /// <summary>
    /// Stream on network card (<c>network.stream_on</c>): Chooses the network card every stream goes out on (Settings › Advanced › Network cards), and a backup for when it's unplugged, gone or without an address: for two internet connections on two cards. auto (the default) is the system's default network. Each stream connection reads the choice as it opens: a change applies from its next connection (a reconnect, or the next Go Live), and a connection that's up is never cut. When the preferred card comes back, streams move to it at their next reconnect. A card that can't be used is never a reason a stream doesn't go out: it falls back to the backup, then to the system's default network, and says why. RTMP, RTMPS, WHIP and VELoQ honor it; SRT follows the system's default network for now (the stream says so).
    /// </summary>
    NetworkStreamOn,
    /// <summary>
    /// Check for updates (<c>update.check</c>): Asks Studio's update channel whether there's a newer Studio, as Check for updates in the Help menu does, and returns once it knows. Nothing is downloaded.
    /// </summary>
    UpdateCheck,
    /// <summary>
    /// Install update (<c>update.install</c>): Downloads the update on offer and checks its signature and checksum, ready to restart into, as Update now does. Refused while live, recording or keeping a replay buffer: updates never start then. With restart, Studio restarts into the new version once it's ready.
    /// </summary>
    UpdateInstall,
    /// <summary>
    /// Raw request (<c>raw.transaction</c>): The escape hatch: commits scene-model commands as one transaction, all or nothing, exactly as Studio's own panels do (see the model's command reference). Changes made this way aren't undo steps.
    /// </summary>
    RawTransaction,
}

